; The command line — the only module tree with I/O besides the LSP process. It
; links all three engine libraries: the data model, the front end (to read
; models), and the interrogator (to answer questions about them). The exe main
; module is [Writ], which is only the argument dispatch: one [Cmd_*] sibling per
; verb, [Cli_io] for the filesystem they share, [Help] for the `--help` text,
; and [Version] generated below. One module per verb so that adding a verb adds
; a file rather than growing the one everything already lives in.

; (public_name writ) is what makes `opam install .` put this binary on the PATH
; as `writ`; without it the exe exists only inside _build.

; The verbs are a LIBRARY and [Writ] is a thin executable over it. An
; executable's modules cannot be linked into a test, so with everything in one
; stanza the only way to check the command line was to run the binary and read
; its output — which is a fine end-to-end test and a poor unit one. UNWRAPPED,
; as [writ_json] is, so the module names stay [Cli_io], [Help] and [Cmd_*] and
; no source file needed an edit to make the split.

(library
 (name writ_cli)
 (wrapped false)
 (modules
  cli_io
  cmd_check
  cmd_query
  cmd_compare
  cmd_control
  cmd_schema
  cmd_derive
  cmd_show
  cmd_sql
  help
  writ_dispatch)
 (libraries writ_data writ_syntax writ_runtime writ_loadpath writ_sql))

(executable
 (name writ)
 (public_name writ)
 (package writ)
 (modules writ version)
 (libraries writ_cli))

; `writ --version` reads the version dune already knows — the one in dune-project
; that opam publishes and `make release` names the tarball with. Generated
; rather than written down, because a hand-kept copy of a version number is a
; copy that is eventually wrong, and a binary lying about which writ it is
; wastes far more time than it saves.

; THE LEADING `v` IS STRIPPED, and the reason is `dune subst`. writ.opam runs
; it in dev mode — which is every `opam pin`, the install route this project
; lists FIRST — and it rewrites dune-project's version from `git describe`. At
; the tag v0.2.0 that is the string "v0.2.0", so a pinned install printed
; `writ v0.2.0` while the tarball, the image and this checkout all printed
; `writ 0.2.0`: one release, two answers to "which writ is this".
;
; opam already strips the v for its own metadata (writ.opam gets 0.2.0), so
; before this the binary disagreed with the package it was installed from,
; inside a single install. Stripping here makes every route agree again, and
; leaves a dev pin honest — it reports 0.1.0-68-gb054efa, which is not a
; release version and does not pretend to be one.
;
; Only before a DIGIT: a version that genuinely began with a letter would be
; left alone.

(rule
 (targets version.ml)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   version.ml
   (echo
    "let v =\n  let raw = \"%{version:writ}\" in\n  if String.length raw > 1 && raw.[0] = 'v' && raw.[1] >= '0' && raw.[1] <= '9'\n  then String.sub raw 1 (String.length raw - 1)\n  else raw\n"))))
